Slice 073, R-B10 (entry `commands`): an `x` cache entry has the binary when its dir holds the file that the manifest names: the repo of the full name, with `.exe` on Windows only (R-X2). One home with the compiler `nightly-2026-10-14-38a3029`, the platform in the package cache, `TALON_NOW=2026-10-15T12:00:00Z`.
Each run is `install --frozen` of a project with one tool whose `url` is locked. The `x` cache has the entry of that tool, but its dir holds the file with the name of the other system (the dir is the real digest of the build, so the new build replaces it: the old dir is deleted, then the temp dir is renamed to it). The fake `platform` has one row for each run: run 1 is Linux, run 2 is Windows.

| Run | Words | Result | Rules |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | `install --frozen --json --file p12/main.roc` | Linux. The entry of acme/foo 1.0.0 has `foo.exe` and `manifest.json`: `foo.exe` is not the file of Linux, so the entry has no binary. The tool is built (`built tool f`), the old dir is deleted, the new dir has `foo`. With `--json`: `data.tools` has one row `built` (the human text goes to stderr) | R-B10, R-X2, R-PT6, R-PT5, R-OUT4 |
| 2 | `install --frozen --file p13/main.roc` | Windows. The entry of acme/bar 1.0.0 (target `x64win`) has `bar` and `manifest.json`: `bar` is not the file of Windows, so it has no binary. The tool is built, the new dir has `bar.exe` | R-B10, R-X2, R-PT6 |

Slice 088b (test world, 2026-11-02, R-LY5 to R-LY7): the fake Windows has `LOCALAPPDATA` and `APPDATA` in its env (HOME form of the case: `{w}/home` or the `u<n>` dir), so the roots are `<LOCALAPPDATA>\roc` (cache), `<LOCALAPPDATA>\talon` (data) and `<APPDATA>\talon` (config). In the case they are the dirs `AppData/Local\roc`, `AppData/Local\talon` and `AppData/Roaming\talon` (names with `\`, in `AppData/`); the packages dir is `AppData/Local\roc\packages`. The files and the paths in `expect/` follow this. The meaning of the runs did not change.

Slice 088f (R-LY10, Q216): `HOME`, `LOCALAPPDATA` and `APPDATA` of the fake Windows are absolute on Windows: `C:/` (HOME of a case with one home), `C:/AppData/Local`, `C:/AppData/Roaming` (with the `u<n>` home: `C:/u1/...`). They are real paths on the Linux and macOS host: `tests/commands/run.sh` makes the link `<W>/C:` to `home` for a case with `C:/` in `fx/`, and runs the app in `<W>`. So `C:/AppData/Local\talon` is the dir `home/AppData/Local\talon`, and the log has the portable text `C:/...` (no work dir path). A run of another system in the same case (the `fx/platform` rows) keeps its own env.
